        <p>Read the take-home column, not the gross column, when you compare. The $58-to-$59 step keeps only $1,444.88, the first step on this ladder that keeps less than the flat $1,463.28, and the next row shows why the ladder stays lower from here: the $59-to-$60 step keeps only $1,421.68, because the whole raise is taxed at 24% plus 7.65% FICA. Against salaries, $59 an hour beats a $115,000 salary by $7,720.00 gross and $5,412.62 of take-home, more than double the $5,640.00 gross and $3,967.74 of take-home that $58 managed against the same salary. The $60,000 salary is the clearest win for the hourly job: $59 an hour beats it by $62,720.00 gross and $44,755.12 of take-home, and no realistic benefits package closes a gap that size. And the $120,000 row is now a clear lead, not a close call: $2,720.00 on gross and $1,895.12 on take-home. Our <a href="/articles/hourly-vs-salary-which-pays-more/">hourly-versus-salary comparison</a> prices any two offers head to head on the same paid-weeks basis, and our <a href="/calculators/salary-comparison/">salary-comparison calculator</a> runs any two offers through the same paid-weeks math.</p>
